{
  "id": "four-chords-worldwide",
  "title": "Four chords, three styles",
  "cues": [
    {
      "id": "intro",
      "anchor": {
        "segment": 0,
        "offset": 0.3
      },
      "kind": "intro",
      "text": "Four chords, three styles. Bob Marley and the Wailers built No Woman, No Cry on one, five, six minor, four. Here that is C, G, A minor, F. Though Marley is widely thought to have written it, the credit went to Vincent Ford, who ran a soup kitchen in Trenchtown.",
      "image": "artist-Q2525354",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "No Woman, No Cry (Wikipedia)",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/No_Woman,_No_Cry"
        },
        {
          "title": "Bob Marley, No Woman, No Cry (Hooktheory TheoryTab, chords)",
          "url": "https://www.hooktheory.com/theorytab/view/bob-marley/no-woman-no-cry"
        },
        {
          "title": "I–V–vi–IV progression (Wikipedia)",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I%E2%80%93V%E2%80%93vi%E2%80%93IV_progression"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "spice-girls-arrive",
      "anchor": {
        "segment": 1,
        "offset": 2.2
      },
      "kind": "changeover",
      "text": "The Spice Girls now sing over Marley's band. Wannabe uses the same four chords, starting on the five.",
      "image": "artist-Q55641",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Spice Girls, Wannabe (Hooktheory TheoryTab, chords)",
          "url": "https://www.hooktheory.com/theorytab/view/spice-girls/wannabe"
        },
        {
          "title": "I–V–vi–IV progression (Wikipedia)",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I%E2%80%93V%E2%80%93vi%E2%80%93IV_progression"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "wannabe",
      "anchor": {
        "segment": 1,
        "offset": 11.1
      },
      "kind": "song",
      "text": "The group wrote it with Matt Rowe and Richard Stannard in about half an hour, stitching chants, raps and sung parts together.",
      "image": "artist-Q55641",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Wannabe (song) (Wikipedia)",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wannabe_(song)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "shakira-arrives",
      "anchor": {
        "segment": 3,
        "offset": 2.2
      },
      "kind": "changeover",
      "text": "Shakira's Whenever, Wherever now rides the Wannabe band. She wrote it first in Spanish, as Suerte, composing with Tim Mitchell.",
      "image": "artist-Q34424",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Whenever, Wherever (Wikipedia)",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whenever,_Wherever"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "outro",
      "anchor": {
        "segment": 4,
        "offset": 0.4
      },
      "kind": "outro",
      "text": "Gloria Estefan helped adapt it into English. Its loop, C sharp minor, A, E, B, is the axis again, begun on the minor chord. Three styles, one loop.",
      "image": "artist-Q34424",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Whenever, Wherever (Wikipedia)",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whenever,_Wherever"
        },
